---
// Splunk SPL Query Builder — compose a base search plus a chain of pipe
// commands and copy the assembled SPL. Same page shape as the other
// interactive tools (intro aside -> tool -> reference notes -> related ->
// external resources). Static page — prerender to HTML served from the CDN.
export const prerender = true;
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import SplBuilder from '../../components/SplBuilder.astro';
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro';
import { toolChip } from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import ExternalResources from '../../components/ExternalResources.astro';
import { EXTERNAL_RESOURCES } from '../../data/externalResources';
import { SPL_AGG_FUNCTIONS, SPL_PIPE_COMMANDS, SPL_PRESETS, SPL_SEARCH_OPERATORS, buildSplQuery } from '../../utils/spl';

// Every query string printed on this page is assembled by the same function
// the tool itself calls — never hand-typed — and each result is asserted
// character-for-character in test/spl.test.ts, so a wrong example can't ship.
const workedExamples = SPL_PRESETS.map((p) => ({ ...p, query: buildSplQuery(p.spec) }));

const sectionH2 = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.6rem',
  fontSize: '1.3rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  mt: '2.5rem',
  mb: '1rem',
  pt: '1.5rem',
  borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  scrollMarginTop: '90px',
});
const refTable = css({ width: '100%', borderCollapse: 'collapse', fontSize: '0.85rem' });
const refTh = css({
  textAlign: 'left',
  color: 'textMuted',
  fontFamily: 'sans',
  fontWeight: 600,
  fontSize: '0.72rem',
  textTransform: 'uppercase',
  letterSpacing: '0.06em',
  p: '0.5rem 0.7rem',
  borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
});
const refTdBase = { p: '0.5rem 0.7rem', borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)', color: 'text', verticalAlign: 'top' } as const;
const refTd = css(refTdBase);
const refTdMuted = css({ ...refTdBase, color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.8rem' });
const refTdCode = css(refTdBase, { fontFamily: 'mono', fontSize: '0.78rem', color: 'primary', whiteSpace: 'nowrap' });
const relatedList = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '0.85rem' });
const relatedReason = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.85rem', mt: '0.35rem', lineHeight: 1.6 });
const bodyText = css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '1rem', mb: '1.5rem' });
const mutedNote = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.88rem', mt: '1.5rem', lineHeight: 1.7 });
const inlineCode = css({ fontFamily: 'mono', fontSize: '0.85rem', color: 'primary' });
const linkStyle = css({ color: 'primary' });

const exampleList = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '1.25rem' });
const exampleTitle = css({ fontSize: '1rem', fontWeight: 700, color: 'text', m: 0 });
const exampleDesc = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.88rem', lineHeight: 1.7, mt: '0.3rem', mb: '0.55rem' });
const exampleCode = css({
  m: 0,
  p: '0.7rem 0.85rem',
  fontFamily: 'mono',
  fontSize: '0.8rem',
  lineHeight: 1.7,
  color: 'text',
  bg: 'codeBg',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap',
  wordBreak: 'break-word',
  maxH: '12rem',
  overflowY: 'auto',
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Splunk SPL Query Builder"
  description="Compose a Splunk SPL search step by step — a base search plus stats, table, sort, where, eval, and head — and copy the assembled query. Syntax verified against Splunk's own Search Reference; runs entirely in your browser."
>
  <PageHeader
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Tool Catalog', href: '/tools/' }, { label: 'Splunk SPL Query Builder' }]}
    eyebrow="Query Builders"
    title="Splunk SPL Query Builder"
    description="Build a Splunk search a stage at a time — index and filters first, then the pipe commands — and copy the assembled SPL to run in your own environment."
  />

  <aside
    class={css({
      bg: 'bgSubtle',
      border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
      borderLeftWidth: '3px',
      borderLeftColor: 'primary',
      borderRadius: 'md',
      p: '1rem 1.25rem',
      mb: '1.5rem',
    })}
  >
    <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.7, fontSize: '0.95rem', m: 0 })}>
      This builder <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>writes SPL, it doesn't run it</strong> — there's no connection to any
      Splunk instance here, no search is executed, and nothing you type leaves your browser. It assembles query text for
      you to paste into your own search bar. It covers a defined subset of SPL — a base search plus six pipe commands —
      each verified against{' '}
      <a
        href="https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/search/spl-search-reference/10.2/quick-reference/command-quick-reference"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class={linkStyle}
      >
        Splunk's own Search Reference
      </a>, not the whole language.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class={bodyText}>
    SPL is the query language behind Splunk, and it reads left to right as a pipeline: a base search narrows the events
    down, then each <code class={inlineCode}>|</code> hands that result set to the next command. That structure is what
    makes it worth building deliberately rather than typing from memory — most of the mistakes that cost an analyst time
    aren't syntax errors, they're ordering errors. Filter after you aggregate and the field you wanted is already gone;
    forget a time range and a <Term slug="triage">triage</Term> query turns into an all-time scan of every index your role
    can reach. This tool makes the pipeline visible: each stage is a card you can enable, fill in, and move, and the
    assembled query updates as you go.
  </p>

  <p class={bodyText}>
    The DFIR use for that is direct. Splunk is one of the <Term slug="siem">SIEM</Term> platforms an investigation's{' '}
    <Term slug="log-aggregation">log aggregation</Term> tends to land in, and the queries that matter during an incident
    are mostly the same handful of shapes: count one event code by source to spot a spray, aggregate process launches to
    find the rare parent, compare two fields against each other,{' '}
    <Term slug="threat-hunting">hunt</Term> for the outlier rather than the known-bad. Those shapes are worth knowing
    cold — the field names change with every deployment, but the pipeline doesn't.
  </p>

  <h2 id="builder" class={css({ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '0.6rem', fontSize: '1.3rem', fontWeight: 700, mb: '1rem', scrollMarginTop: '90px' })}>
    <Icon name="terminal" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Query builder
  </h2>
  <SplBuilder />

  <h2 id="commands" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="list" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Supported pipe commands
  </h2>
  <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })}>
    <table class={refTable}>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class={refTh}>Command</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Documented syntax</th>
          <th class={refTh}>What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SPL_PIPE_COMMANDS.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td class={refTd}>
                <a href={c.docUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class={css({ color: 'primary', fontWeight: 600 })}>
                  {c.id}
                </a>
              </td>
              <td class={refTdCode}>{c.syntax}</td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>{c.summary}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class={mutedNote}>
    Command names link to their page in Splunk's Search Reference. The syntax column is the documented form this builder
    emits, not the command's complete argument list — <code class={inlineCode}>stats</code>, for instance, also takes{' '}
    <code class={inlineCode}>partitions</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>allnum</code>, and <code class={inlineCode}>delim</code>{' '}
    arguments that this tool doesn't expose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="aggregations" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="activity" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Aggregation functions in stats
  </h2>
  <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })}>
    <table class={refTable}>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class={refTh}>Function</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Syntax</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Behavior</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SPL_AGG_FUNCTIONS.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <td class={refTd}>
                <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>{f.label}</strong>
              </td>
              <td class={refTdCode}>{f.syntax}</td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>{f.hint}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class={mutedNote}>
    <code class={inlineCode}>count</code> is the only one of these Splunk's own examples write bare — every other function
    takes a field in parentheses, and the builder flags it as an error if you leave that field empty rather than quietly
    dropping the aggregation. Splunk has many more aggregate and multivalue functions than these six; they're out of this
    tool's scope rather than partially supported. Add an <code class={inlineCode}>AS</code> name to any of them to rename
    the resulting column — the builder quotes that name for you if it contains a space.
  </p>

  <h2 id="filters" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="crosshair" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Base-search comparison operators
  </h2>
  <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })}>
    <table class={refTable}>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class={refTh}>Operator</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Behavior</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SPL_SEARCH_OPERATORS.map((op) => (
            <tr>
              <td class={refTdCode}>field{op.id}value</td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>{op.hint}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class={mutedNote}>
    Splunk documents <code class={inlineCode}>AND</code> as implied between terms, so the builder space-separates your
    filters rather than writing the operator out. Boolean operators, when you do write them by hand, have to be uppercase.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quoting" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="key" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Quoting, escaping, and where vs. search
  </h2>
  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '0.95rem', mb: '1rem' })}>
    Two rules cause most of the confusion in hand-written SPL, and the builder applies both for you so you can type the
    literal value you want to match:
  </p>
  <ul class={css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '0.7rem', color: 'text', fontSize: '0.92rem', lineHeight: 1.75, pl: '1.2rem', mb: '1rem' })}>
    <li>
      <strong>Quoting and escaping.</strong> Splunk's docs call for quotation marks around values containing white space,
      commas, pipes, quotation marks, or brackets — so the builder quotes exactly those and leaves everything else bare,
      including wildcards, which stay unquoted the way Splunk's own <code class={inlineCode}>host=webserver*</code>{' '}
      example writes them. A literal backslash is escaped as <code class={inlineCode}>\\</code> and a literal double quote
      as <code class={inlineCode}>\"</code>, which is why a Windows path you type as
      <code class={inlineCode}>C:\Windows</code> comes out as <code class={inlineCode}>C:\\Windows</code>. Note that a
      backslash can't escape an asterisk in SPL — Splunk documents that explicitly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code class={inlineCode}>where</code> reads bare names as fields.</strong> This is the difference between the
      two filtering commands. In the base search, <code class={inlineCode}>ipaddress=clientip</code> matches the literal
      string "clientip"; in <code class={inlineCode}>where</code>, the same expression compares the{' '}
      <em>two fields</em> against each other, and a string literal has to be double-quoted to be read as one. The
      builder's <code class={inlineCode}>where</code> stage makes that an explicit choice — text, number, or field —
      rather than something you find out from an empty result set. Field names get single quotes when Splunk requires
      them: a name starting with a digit, or containing anything outside letters, digits, and underscores.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="time" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="clock" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Time modifiers
  </h2>
  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '0.95rem', mb: '1rem' })}>
    <code class={inlineCode}>earliest</code> and <code class={inlineCode}>latest</code> take Splunk's relative-time format:
    an optional sign, an integer, a unit, and an optional <code class={inlineCode}>@</code> snap that rounds down to the
    start of that unit. So <code class={inlineCode}>-24h@h</code> means "24 hours ago, snapped back to the top of the
    hour", and <code class={inlineCode}>now</code> is its own literal. Units run from <code class={inlineCode}>s</code>{' '}
    through <code class={inlineCode}>m</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>h</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>d</code>,{' '}
    <code class={inlineCode}>w</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>mon</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>q</code>, and{' '}
    <code class={inlineCode}>y</code>, each with longer spellings Splunk also accepts. Splunk's docs recommend pairing a
    relative <code class={inlineCode}>earliest</code> with <code class={inlineCode}>latest=now</code> to avoid an
    inaccurate window.
  </p>
  <p class={mutedNote}>
    These two values are passed through <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>exactly as typed</strong> — never quoted or
    rewritten — because Splunk also accepts absolute timestamps here, and reformatting one would mean guessing at your
    intent. When a value doesn't match the relative-time grammar, the builder says so as a note rather than silently
    "fixing" it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="examples" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="file-text" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Worked examples
  </h2>
  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '0.95rem', mb: '1.25rem' })}>
    Each of these loads into the builder from the dropdown at the top of the tool. They're here to show the{' '}
    <em>shape</em> of a query, not to be pasted as-is: the index, sourcetype, and field names below follow the conventions
    Splunk's own Windows and web add-ons create, but every deployment onboards data differently, so treat them as starting
    points to adapt to your own field names.
  </p>
  <div class={exampleList}>
    {
      workedExamples.map((ex) => (
        <div>
          <h3 class={exampleTitle}>{ex.label}</h3>
          <p class={exampleDesc}>{ex.description}</p>
          <pre class={exampleCode}><code>{ex.query}</code></pre>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="scope" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> What this tool deliberately doesn't do
  </h2>
  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '0.95rem', mb: '1rem' })}>
    These are scope decisions, not gaps waiting to be filled in — worth stating plainly so you know exactly what you're
    getting:
  </p>
  <ul class={css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '0.55rem', color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.9rem', lineHeight: 1.7, pl: '1.2rem' })}>
    <li>
      <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>It doesn't run searches.</strong> No Splunk connection, no credentials, no
      results — it produces text.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>It doesn't read SPL.</strong> There's no parser, so you can't paste an
      existing query in and have the form fill itself out. The data flow is one-way.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>It doesn't validate free-text expressions.</strong> An{' '}
      <code class={inlineCode}>eval</code> expression is emitted verbatim. Checking it properly would mean implementing
      Splunk's full eval grammar, and a half-implementation that quietly accepts a broken expression would be worse than
      none.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>It covers six pipe commands.</strong> Subsearches, macros, lookups,{' '}
      <code class={inlineCode}>tstats</code> and data models, <code class={inlineCode}>transaction</code>,{' '}
      <code class={inlineCode}>rex</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>timechart</code>, <code class={inlineCode}>dedup</code>,{' '}
      <code class={inlineCode}>top</code>/<code class={inlineCode}>rare</code>, and SPL2 syntax are all out of scope.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>One card per command.</strong> The pipeline holds at most one of each
      command, in whatever order you arrange them — real SPL happily uses <code class={inlineCode}>eval</code> three times
      in a row, and this doesn't.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="related" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="wrench" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Related tools
  </h2>
  <div class={relatedList}>
    <div>
      <a href="/tools/sigma-tester/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="target" size={13} /> Sigma Rule Tester <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        Sigma is the vendor-neutral way to write the same detection logic once and convert it to a backend's own syntax —
        the complement to writing SPL directly. Build the rule there, hand-write the search here.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <a href="/event-ids/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="list" size={13} /> Windows Event ID Reference <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        Looking up which event code to filter on before you build the search — 4625 for a failed logon, Sysmon 1 for
        process creation, and what each one actually records.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <a href="/tools/regex-tester/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="search" size={13} /> Regex Tester <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        SPL's own regex commands are outside this builder's scope, but if you're writing a pattern to drop into one,
        validate it against sample text first.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <a href="/tools/ioc-extractor/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="crosshair" size={13} /> IOC Extractor <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        Pull the indicators out of a report first, then drop them into a base-search filter here to check whether they
        ever appear in your own data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ExternalResources resources={EXTERNAL_RESOURCES['spl-builder']} />
</BaseLayout>
